{"packet_id":"D067-03","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act just cause eviction grounds","requirement":"A landlord may evict a residential tenant only for the statutory grounds in the Anti-Eviction Act, such as nonpayment, disorderly conduct, damage, lease violations, health and safety violations, retirement from the rental market, and conversion, with the notice each ground requires.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Applies to most residential rentals: single-family homes, mobile homes and mobile home park land, apartment buildings, complexes, and rooming and boarding homes.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"owner","role":"exempt","who":"owner-occupied","unit_limit":3}],"per_tenancy":"A unit held in trust for a developmentally disabled immediate-family member who permanently occupies it is excluded.","coverage_quotes":["This law may not apply to two - or three-unit owner-occupied premises with two (2) or fewer rental units.","Additionally, this law does not apply to a unit held in trust on behalf of a member of the immediate family, if that family member is developmentally disabled, and permanently occupies the dwelling unit."]},"exemptions":"Two- or three-unit owner-occupied premises with two or fewer rental units; hotel and motel guests and transient or seasonal guest house tenants (unless the occupant has no other residence and stays continually); a unit held in trust for a developmentally disabled immediate-family member who permanently occupies it.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"If a tenant fails to pay rent, the landlord may immediately take legal action to have the tenant evicted. The landlord is not required to give the tenant notice before filing an eviction suit, except if the tenant resides in federally subsidized housing.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-03","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Prohibition on self-help evictions and lockouts","requirement":"A landlord may not evict a tenant by self-help, such as padlocking, changing locks, disconnecting utilities, blocking entry or removing belongings; removal requires a court judgment and a warrant enforced by a court officer.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All residential rental premises in New Jersey.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"A landlord who illegally evicts a tenant may be liable for damages, including possession of the unit, court costs and reasonable attorney fees, and may be charged with disorderly conduct.","effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:39-1","quoted_span":"A landlord or their agent may not padlock, disconnect utilities or otherwise block entry to a rental premise while the tenant still lives there.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-03","n_rules":2,"note":null}

